WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:04.420
Leila runs a repair workshop
at a fictional train station.

2
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.340
Two AI assistants help her.

3
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.756
This morning, the first one fixes
a broken timetable display.

4
00:00:10.756 --> 00:00:12.866
Before it moves on, it leaves a note:

5
00:00:12.866 --> 00:00:15.096
which version of the display it was,

6
00:00:15.096 --> 00:00:17.872
what it tried,
and how it checked the fix.

7
00:00:18.062 --> 00:00:22.672
An hour later, the second assistant finds
a display failing in a similar way.

8
00:00:22.672 --> 00:00:25.556
Should it solve the same problem
from scratch?

9
00:00:25.846 --> 00:00:28.986
That’s the idea behind
Stack Overflow for Agents.

10
00:00:28.986 --> 00:00:32.376
In June, Stack Overflow announced it as a beta:

11
00:00:32.376 --> 00:00:36.134
a place where AI agents
can share what they’ve learned.

12
00:00:36.224 --> 00:00:38.064
It’s built around an API.

13
00:00:38.064 --> 00:00:42.551
That’s a doorway one piece of software
uses to ask another for something.

14
00:00:42.641 --> 00:00:45.711
So an agent can search the shared notes
before it starts,

15
00:00:45.711 --> 00:00:48.373
instead of rediscovering a known fix.

16
00:00:48.563 --> 00:00:51.703
Think of it as a shared notebook,
not a shared brain.

17
00:00:51.703 --> 00:00:54.413
Reading a note
doesn’t retrain the assistant.

18
00:00:54.413 --> 00:00:56.553
It just gives it something to check.

19
00:00:56.743 --> 00:00:58.023
And checking matters.

20
00:00:58.023 --> 00:01:00.203
This display is a different version.

21
00:01:00.203 --> 00:01:03.643
A copied recipe might not fit
different ingredients.

22
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:05.823
So the assistant compares the context,

23
00:01:05.823 --> 00:01:08.323
tries the fix, and reviews the result.

24
00:01:08.323 --> 00:01:10.043
Then it adds what happened:

25
00:01:10.043 --> 00:01:11.983
what worked here, and what didn’t.

26
00:01:12.173 --> 00:01:16.073
Stack Overflow says checking answers,
not just posting them,

27
00:01:16.073 --> 00:01:18.153
is what earns reputation there.

28
00:01:18.153 --> 00:01:22.183
And an agent can show its draft
to a person before it’s published.

29
00:01:22.273 --> 00:01:25.663
In September, it added playbooks
for step-by-step know-how,

30
00:01:25.663 --> 00:01:29.503
privacy and publishing controls,
and a read-only mode.

31
00:01:29.593 --> 00:01:33.543
It also says it’s still checking
how valuable this kind of sharing is.

32
00:01:33.543 --> 00:01:36.423
A shared answer
isn’t proof that it’s right.

33
00:01:36.613 --> 00:01:38.593
Leila keeps one rule of her own:

34
00:01:38.593 --> 00:01:41.923
private project logs
never go in the shared notebook.

35
00:01:42.113 --> 00:01:45.583
When an assistant brings back a shared fix,
ask three things.

36
00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:46.683
What was tried?

37
00:01:46.683 --> 00:01:47.633
Where did it work?

38
00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:49.183
And how was it checked?

39
00:01:49.473 --> 00:01:52.323
A shared note is a starting point,
not a verdict.
